            <ArticleTitle>Atherosclerosis in type 2 diabetes mellitus: the role of insulin resistance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>253-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Type 2 diabetes mellitus is associated with a marked increase in the risk of atherosclerotic diseases, including coronary heart disease, peripheral arterial disease, and cerebrovascular disease. Insulin resistance is a key factor in the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Insulin resistance and its attendant metabolic abnormalities may cause much of the increased cardiovascular risk of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Among the abnormalities associated with insulin resistance are dyslipidemia, hypertension, systemic inflammation, and a prothrombotic state. This review discusses the role that each of these disorders plays in the cardiovascular risk of type 2 diabetes mellitus.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
